  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given a binary string SSS.\n\nIn one operation, you can pick an index iii (1≤i&lt;∣S∣)(1\\le i \\lt |S|)(1≤i&lt;∣S∣) and swap the characters SiS_iSi​ and S(i+1)S_{(i+1)}S(i+1)​.\n\nFind the **minimum** number of operations required, such that, the decimal representation of the final binary string is **divisible** by 333. If it is impossible to do so, print −1-1−1 instead.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of single line of input, containing a binary string SSS.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the **minimum** number of operations required, such that, the decimal representation of the final binary string is **divisible** by 333. If it is impossible to do so, print −1-1−1 instead.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤∣S∣≤3⋅1051 \\leq |S| \\leq 3\\cdot 10^51≤∣S∣≤3⋅105",
      "SSS consists of 000 and 111 only.",
      "The sum of ∣S∣|S|∣S∣ over all test cases won't exceed 3⋅1053\\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n0000\n111\n11001",
        "output": "0\n-1\n1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: There is no need to apply any operation since the decimal representation of 000000000000 is 000 which is divisible by 333.\nTest case 222: It can be shown that we cannot make the decimal representation of the string divisible by 333 using any number of operations.\nTest case 333: The decimal representation of 110011100111001 is 252525 . Using one operation, we pick i=2i = 2i=2 and swap S2S_2S2​ and S3S_3S3​. Thus, the string becomes 101011010110101, whose decimal representation is 212121, which is divisible by 333."
      }
    ]
  },
